<h2>The accident and the course below</h2>
<p>On 22 October 2016, near the Paliyekkara toll plaza on the Thrissur&ndash;Kodara road, a scooter carrying the two claimants collided with another two-wheeler. Sunil Kumar, the 43-year-old pillion rider, suffered severe head injuries — an open diffuse axonal injury and midbrain contusion with Weber&rsquo;s syndrome — along with a penetrating knee injury. The medical board certified 75.5% disability. Sreejith K.S., the 22-year-old rider, sustained multiple fractures of the wrist and hand and a brachial plexus injury, certified at 62%.</p>
<p>The Tribunal at Irinjalakuda pegged Sunil Kumar&rsquo;s income at ₹9,000 per month on the strength of an agricultural training certificate and awarded ₹19.91 lakh. The Kerala High Court raised the income to ₹15,000 and the award to ₹36.22 lakh. For Sreejith, the Tribunal&rsquo;s ₹16.03 lakh became ₹27.70 lakh on a notional income of ₹10,500. Both claimants pressed on to the Supreme Court.</p>

<h2>From 75.5% on paper to 100% in life</h2>
<p>Relying on its decisions in <em>Shankar Dutt</em>, <em>Jithendran</em> and <em>Reliance General Insurance v. Priyanka Das</em>, the Court held that the medical percentage understated the true impact. Sunil Kumar depends on others for the activities of daily living, requires an attendant and bystander for life, and has lost his working capacity altogether. His functional disability was therefore fixed at 100%. Sreejith, whose dominant-hand and plexus injuries ended his capacity for manual work, was assessed at 80%.</p>

<h2>The recomputed award</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Head</th><th>Sunil Kumar (₹)</th><th>Sreejith K.S. (₹)</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Loss of earnings (laid-up period)</td><td>1,80,000</td><td>1,26,000</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Attendant / bystander (multiplier method)</td><td>25,20,000</td><td>22,68,000</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Medical expenses (incurred)</td><td>4,02,394</td><td>1,94,720</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Future medical expenses</td><td>10,00,000</td><td>8,00,000</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Loss of earning power (with future prospects)</td><td>31,50,000</td><td>25,40,160</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Pain and suffering</td><td>2,50,000</td><td>2,50,000</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Loss of amenities</td><td>2,50,000</td><td>2,50,000</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Loss of marriage prospects</td><td>&mdash;</td><td>5,00,000</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Transport, nourishment, clothing &amp; vehicle damage</td><td>32,000</td><td>32,000</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td><strong>77,84,394</strong></td><td><strong>69,60,880</strong></td></tr>
</table>
<p>The enhanced amounts — ₹41.61 lakh and ₹41.90 lakh over the High Court figures — carry interest at 6.5% per annum from the date of the claim petitions. The insurer must deposit the sums within six weeks, and the Tribunal is to disburse by direct bank transfer after verifying identity.</p>

<h2>Lessons for claim preparation</h2>
<div class="check">
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Prove the vocation, not just the injury.</strong> The gap between ₹9,000 as a notional farmer and the claimed ₹30,000 as a bakery trader shows how income evidence shapes the award at every level.</li>
    <li><strong>Capitalise the attendant.</strong> Where the need is lifelong, attendant charges computed on the multiplier method can rival the loss-of-earnings head itself — here they exceeded ₹25 lakh.</li>
    <li><strong>Future medical costs are a distinct head.</strong> Neurological injuries justified ₹10 lakh for future treatment, separate from the expenses already incurred.</li>
    <li><strong>Young victims have additional heads.</strong> For an unmarried 22-year-old, loss of marriage prospects was separately compensated.</li>
  </ul>
</div>
<div class="note"><p>This article is for general information only and is not legal advice. Compensation in motor accident claims depends on the evidence in each case, and the judgment should be read in full before being relied upon.</p></div>
